/home/pixel/code/htb-2026/docs/product.md:22:- LLM calls and AI agents: Vercel AI SDK in the web app, through Vercel AI Gateway, for both the competitor-campaign agent and the Jev calls. The Python scripts in `tools/` call no language model. Python prepares the numerical dataset, and everything that depends on one startup's form runs in the web app. 2026-11-07.
/home/pixel/code/htb-2026/docs/product.md:23:- Audience fit scoring: Jev, TypeSafe AI's decision model, scores the target-customer sentence against each column of the prebuilt tract dataset, and the web app weights the columns by those scores. Jev answers declared yes/no, choice and score questions with probabilities instead of writing text, and is called as `typesafe-ai/jev` through AI Gateway. The tract data is numbers, which code combines without a model, so Jev's part is turning the sentence into weights. Weights are signed, because the direction depends on the customer: a high income is good for an affluent audience and bad for a low-income one, and `median_age` points the opposite way for young and older audiences. The weighted score divides by the sum of the absolute weights, because signed weights can sum to zero and make a plain average unstable. One request costs about $0.00001. 2026-11-07.
/home/pixel/code/htb-2026/docs/product.md:33:- The web app runs each startup's result live when the form is submitted, so a startup typed in at the demo gets its own map. Only the city dataset is prepared ahead of time. Jev and the agent run in Node.js inside the web app, so no Python runs behind it. 2026-11-07.
/home/pixel/code/htb-2026/docs/product.md:34:- How the web app's server does the work: a Server Function takes the form, reads the tract data and column list from `data/`, calls Jev, and returns an audience fit score per tract ID. The browser loads the tract shapes once, not on every submission. The competitor-campaign agent runs in a Route Handler that streams each step to the page, because a Server Function returns only when it finishes and Next.js runs a client's Server Functions one at a time. The server matches each campaign found to a subway station. The data stays in `data/`, and deploying needs `outputFileTracingRoot` and `outputFileTracingIncludes` in `next.config.ts` so the build includes it. 2026-11-07.
/home/pixel/code/htb-2026/docs/product.md:35:- What the Python analysis writes for the web app, all in `data/`: the tract shapes, and a tract table with each feature column also scaled from 0 to 1 so weighted columns can be added; a column list giving each column's name, a plain description and whether it measures living, working or passing through, from which the web app builds its Jev questions; the placements with their tract; and the subway stations. 2026-11-07.

